.\" Hand-written on purpose, and it lists no options at all.
.\"
.\" The three daemons parse their arguments by hand rather than with clap --
.\" a handful of optional flags does not earn a dependency in a process on the
.\" token path -- so nothing can generate these pages the way kbconfig.1,
.\" kbmanage.1 and kbsetup.8 are generated. A page that names a flag is a page
.\" that goes stale the first time one changes, and nothing fails when it does.
.\" So this
.\" one carries only prose that stays true, and points at --help for the rest
.\" -- a flag the three daemons gained for this, since a hand-rolled parser has
.\" none unless somebody writes one.
.\" `make test` holds it to that.
.TH ISSUERD 8 "KerBridge" "KerBridge" "System Manager's Manual"
.SH NAME
issuerd \- KerBridge issuer daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B issuerd
\fR[\fIoptions\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B issuerd
is the half of KerBridge that holds KDC authority. It runs as root on the Samba
AD domain controller, listens on a unix socket, and answers one question: for
an account the broker has already admitted, produce a Kerberos ticket-granting
ticket.
.PP
It does that with the local Samba tools rather than over the network. For each
request it exports a keytab for the one principal, obtains a credential cache
with it, hands the cache back over the socket, and deletes both. The keytab and
the cache live in a directory that must be on tmpfs, because they are key
material with a lifetime of one request.
.PP
The daemon opens no network listener and makes no outbound connection. Its
entire external surface is five programs \- the Kerberos client, the Samba
administration tool, two LDB tools and
.BR timeout (1)
\- run with a fixed PATH and a cleared environment.
.PP
The socket is the authorization boundary. It is created inside a directory the
daemon owns, and the peer's credentials are checked against the configured
broker account before a single byte is read: only root and that one account are
answered.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.B issuerd
reads the KerBridge configuration set, a directory of TOML files whose default
location is
.IR /etc/kerbridge .
Its own file there is
.IR issuerd.toml ,
and it reads
.I realm.toml
and
.I main.toml
beside it.
.PP
The set is validated before the daemon starts:
.B kbconfig check
runs first and
.B kbsetup verify
after it, both as
.B ExecStartPre
of the unit below. A set that does not check out keeps this one service from
starting, and affects nothing else; systemd retries five times and then leaves
the unit
.BR failed ,
so the reason stays in
.B systemctl status
instead of scrolling past in the journal.
.PP
With no configuration set at all the unit is skipped rather than started:
.B ConditionPathExists=/etc/kerbridge/main.toml
guards it, so an installed-but-unconfigured host is quiet and
.B systemctl start
still exits 0.
.PP
No credential is ever written in the configuration set. Secrets are files under
.IR /etc/kerbridge.secrets/ ,
named in the set as paths.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/kerbridge/issuerd.toml
This daemon's own configuration.
.TP
.I /var/log/kerbridge/issuerd/audit.log
The append-only record of every ticket issued. Rotated by
.IR /etc/logrotate.d/kerbridge-issuerd ,
which signals the daemon to reopen the file rather than truncating it under
its feet. One directory per daemon, so no other KerBridge service can rewrite
this one.
.TP
.I /var/lib/kerbridge/issuerd/
Durable state: the operator-notification conditions currently outstanding.
.TP
.I /etc/krb5.conf.d/kerbridge.conf
The Kerberos client configuration
.B kbsetup realm
generates from the configuration set. It is read because this package's
installation added an
.B includedir
line to
.IR /etc/krb5.conf ;
purging the package removes both, in that order.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGUSR1
Reopen the audit log. Sent by
.BR logrotate (8)
after it has rotated the file, so that the daemon writes to the successor
rather than to the rotated inode. A
.B REOPEN
record in the new file says where the trail continues.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
Nonzero on any startup failure, with the reason on the console. A name in the
configuration set that does not resolve to a local unix user or group is one of
those: the daemon refuses rather than falling back to a number, because a
silent disagreement about that identity costs every login.
.SH OPTIONS
issuerd takes a small and stable set of options, and this page deliberately
names none of them: a hand-written list is a copy, and a copy is what goes
stale the first time an option changes. Run
.B issuerd --help
for the set this build has. An argument it does not recognise is refused rather
than ignored, and the refusal prints the same usage line.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR kbconfig (1),
.BR kbsetup (8),
.BR kerbridge-broker (8),
.BR kerbridge-sync (8),
.BR samba (8),
.BR systemd.exec (5)
.PP
.I /usr/share/doc/kerbridge-issuerd/
holds the firewall examples for the ports a domain controller serves.
.SH AUTHOR
Jarno Elonen.
